Confluence Wiki Markup Instructions

When generating documentation for this task, output the result in Confluence Wiki Markup, not Markdown, HTML, Confluence Storage Format or Atlassian Document Format.

The output must be ready to paste into a system that supports Confluence Wiki Markup.

Output Rules

1. Return only the requested documentation.
2. Do not wrap the complete result in triple backticks.
3. Do not add explanations before or after the documentation.
4. Do not mix Markdown syntax with Confluence Wiki Markup.
5. Preserve all technical details supplied by the user.
6. Do not invent names, URLs, owners, environments, commands or requirements.
7. Use clear placeholders when information is missing.
8. Make sure every opened macro is properly closed.
9. Keep the document structured, concise and easy to scan.
10. Use consistent terminology throughout the document.

Headings

Use Confluence heading syntax:

{noformat}
h1. Page Title

h2. Main Section

h3. Subsection

h4. Smaller Subsection
{noformat}

Rules:

* Use one {{h1.}} heading for the page title.
* Use {{h2.}} for major sections.
* Use {{h3.}} for subsections.
* Do not use Markdown headings such as {{# Title}} or {{## Section}}.
* Do not skip heading levels unless necessary.

Text Formatting

Use:

{noformat}
bold text
italic text
{{inline code}}
-deleted text-
+underlined text+
{noformat}

Examples:

{noformat}
Status: In Progress

Run {{npm install}} before starting the application.

This step is optional.
{noformat}

Use inline code for:

* Commands
* File names
* Variables
* Function names
* Class names
* Configuration keys
* API paths
* Status codes
* Short code expressions

Paragraphs

Separate paragraphs with a blank line.

Use forced line breaks only when necessary:

{noformat}
First line\
Second line
{noformat}

Unordered Lists

Use one or more asterisks:

{noformat}

* Backend
* Frontend
* Database
  {noformat}

Nested list:

{noformat}

* Backend
  ** REST API
  ** Authentication
  ** Database access
* Frontend
  ** Customer application
  ** Admin application
  {noformat}

Ordered Lists

Use one or more hash symbols:

{noformat}

Install the dependencies.

Configure the application.

Run the tests.

Start the service.

{noformat}

Nested ordered list:

{noformat}

Prepare the repository.

Clone the repository.

Create a feature branch.

Implement the change.

Update the code.

Add tests.

{noformat}

Mixed Lists

Example:

{noformat}

Install the required software:

#* Node.js
#* npm
#* Git

Configure the project.

Run the tests.

{noformat}

Avoid nesting lists deeper than three levels.

Links

External link:

{noformat}
[Documentation|https://example.com]
{noformat}

Confluence page:

{noformat}
[Deployment Guide]
{noformat}

Page in another space:

{noformat}
[ARCH:Architecture Guidelines]
{noformat}

Link to a heading:

{noformat}
[Deployment Guide#Rollback Procedure]
{noformat}

Custom label:

{noformat}
[Read the rollback instructions|Deployment Guide#Rollback Procedure]
{noformat}

Email:

{noformat}
[Contact the team|mailto:team@example.com]
{noformat}

Do not use Markdown links such as:

{noformat}
"Documentation" (https://example.com)
{noformat}

Do not invent links.

Tables

Use double pipes for headers and single pipes for normal cells.

Example:

{noformat}
|| Field || Required || Description ||
| Name | Yes | Name of the service |
| Owner | Yes | Team responsible for the service |
| Repository | Yes | Source repository |
| Notes | No | Additional information |
{noformat}

Rules:

* Every row must start and end with a pipe.
* Use {{||}} for header cells.
* Use {{|}} for regular cells.
* Keep the same number of cells in every row.
* Keep table content concise.
* Use tables only for structured information or comparisons.
* Do not use Markdown separator rows such as {{| --- | --- |}}.

Example API table:

{noformat}
|| Method || Path || Description ||
| {{GET}} | {{/api/items}} | Returns all items |
| {{POST}} | {{/api/items}} | Creates an item |
| {{DELETE}} | {{/api/items/{id}}} | Deletes an item |
{noformat}

Code Blocks

Use the Confluence {{code}} macro.

Basic example:

{noformat}
{code}
const message = "Hello";
console.log(message);
{code}
{noformat}

With a language:

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript}
const message = "Hello";
console.log(message);
{code}
{noformat}

With a title:

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript|title=server.js}
import Koa from "koa";

const app = new Koa();
app.listen(3000);
{code}
{noformat}

With line numbers:

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript|linenumbers=true}
function add(a, b) {
return a + b;
}
{code}
{noformat}

Common language identifiers include:

* {{javascript}}
* {{typescript}}
* {{java}}
* {{python}}
* {{bash}}
* {{shell}}
* {{sql}}
* {{json}}
* {{yaml}}
* {{xml}}
* {{html}}
* {{css}}
* {{text}}

Do not use Markdown triple-backtick code fences.

Preserve the original indentation inside code blocks.

No-Format Blocks

Use {{noformat}} for output that should be displayed exactly as written without syntax highlighting.

Example:

{noformat}
{noformat}
SERVICE_NAME=orders-api
PORT=3000
LOG_LEVEL=info
{noformat}
{noformat}

Use no-format blocks for:

* Directory trees
* Log output
* Console output
* Plain configuration
* Templates
* Text containing many markup characters

Quotes

Single paragraph:

{noformat}
bq. All production changes must be reviewed.
{noformat}

Multiple paragraphs:

{noformat}
{quote}
First quoted paragraph.

Second quoted paragraph.
{quote}
{noformat}

Information Panels

Use panels only for meaningful information.

Info:

{noformat}
{info}
This process applies only to production deployments.
{info}
{noformat}

Note:

{noformat}
{note}
The initial deployment may take several minutes.
{note}
{noformat}

Tip:

{noformat}
{tip}
Run the tests locally before opening a pull request.
{tip}
{noformat}

Warning:

{noformat}
{warning}
Do not delete production data.
{warning}
{noformat}

Custom panel:

{noformat}
{panel:title=Important}
Panel content.
{panel}
{noformat}

When macro compatibility is uncertain, use a bold label instead:

{noformat}
Warning: Do not deploy before the migration is complete.
{noformat}

Expandable Sections

When supported:

{noformat}
{expand:title=View implementation details}
Detailed implementation information.
{expand}
{noformat}

Use expandable sections for optional or lengthy information.

Do not place critical instructions only inside an expandable section.

Table of Contents

For long pages:

{noformat}
{toc:minLevel=2|maxLevel=3}
{noformat}

Do not add a table of contents to a short document.

Horizontal Rule

Use:

{noformat}

{noformat}

Use horizontal rules sparingly.

Attachments and Images

Attached image:

{noformat}
!architecture.png!
{noformat}

Image with a width:

{noformat}
!architecture.png|width=800!
{noformat}

Attachment link:

{noformat}
[^deployment-guide.pdf]
{noformat}

Custom attachment label:

{noformat}
[Download the deployment guide^deployment-guide.pdf]
{noformat}

Do not invent attachment names.

Special Characters

Wiki Markup may interpret characters such as:

{noformat}

* _ - + { } [ ] | #
  {noformat}

For technical values, prefer inline code:

{noformat}
{{value_with_underscores}}
{noformat}

For multiline technical text, use a {{code}} or {{noformat}} block.

Writing Style

The documentation must be:

* Clear
* Direct
* Technically accurate
* Well structured
* Easy to scan
* Consistent
* Suitable for technical readers

Use short paragraphs.

Use imperative language for procedures:

* Configure the environment.
* Run the tests.
* Verify the deployment.
* Review the logs.

Use descriptive headings.

Prefer:

{noformat}
h2. Configure Database Credentials
{noformat}

Instead of:

{noformat}
h2. Configuration
{noformat}

Avoid:

* Unnecessary introductions
* Repeated information
* Very long paragraphs
* Excessive formatting
* Deeply nested lists
* Huge tables
* Unsupported macros
* Vague instructions

Define uncommon abbreviations the first time they appear.

Placeholders

When information is missing, use explicit placeholders:

{noformat}
[Service Name]
[Team Name]
[Repository URL]
[Environment Name]
[Insert deployment command]
[Decision Owner]
YYYY-MM-DD
{noformat}

You may also use:

{noformat}
TODO: Add the production dashboard URL.
{noformat}

Never invent:

* Team names
* Owners
* URLs
* Credentials
* Repository names
* Environment names
* Version numbers
* Dates
* API paths
* Commands
* Requirements

Recommended Technical Document Structure

Use this structure when appropriate:

{noformat}
h1. Page Title

Briefly explain the purpose of the page.

{toc:minLevel=2|maxLevel=3}

h2. Overview

Describe the system, feature or process.

h2. Goals

* Goal one
* Goal two

h2. Non-Goals

* Non-goal one
* Non-goal two

h2. Prerequisites

|| Requirement || Description ||
| Access | Required permissions |
| Software | Required tools and versions |
| Configuration | Required environment variables |

h2. Architecture

Describe the components and interactions.

h3. Components

* Component A
* Component B
* Component C

h3. Data Flow

The client sends a request.

The API validates the request.

The service processes the operation.

The database stores the result.

h2. Implementation

Describe the implementation.

h3. Configuration

{code:language=yaml|title=config.yaml}
service:
name: example-service
port: 3000
{code}

h2. Testing

Run the unit tests.

Run the integration tests.

Verify the behavior manually.

h2. Deployment

Build the application.

Deploy to the testing environment.

Verify the deployment.

Deploy to production.

h2. Rollback

Describe how to restore the previous version.

h2. Risks

|| Risk || Impact || Mitigation ||
| Deployment failure | High | Use an automated rollback |
| Invalid configuration | Medium | Validate configuration before deployment |

h2. Open Questions

* Question one
* Question two

h2. References

* [Related Page]
* [External Documentation|https://example.com]
  {noformat}

Adapt the structure to the requested document. Do not include irrelevant or empty sections.

Common Mistakes

Incorrect Markdown heading:

{noformat}

Installation

{noformat}

Correct:

{noformat}
h2. Installation
{noformat}

Incorrect Markdown bold:

{noformat}
Important
{noformat}

Correct:

{noformat}
Important
{noformat}

Incorrect Markdown link:

{noformat}
"Documentation" (https://example.com)
{noformat}

Correct:

{noformat}
[Documentation|https://example.com]
{noformat}

Incorrect Markdown table:

{noformat}

Name| Description
API| Backend service
{noformat}| 

Correct:

{noformat}
|| Name || Description ||
| API | Backend service |
{noformat}

Incorrect Markdown code block:

{noformat}

console.log("Hello");

{noformat}

Correct:

{noformat}
{code:language=javascript}
console.log("Hello");
{code}
{noformat}

Final Validation

Before returning the output, verify:

* Headings use {{h1.}}, {{h2.}} and {{h3.}}.
* There are no Markdown headings.
* Bold text uses single asterisks.
* Inline code uses double curly braces.
* Code blocks use the {{code}} macro.
* Tables use double pipes for headers.
* Links use Confluence link syntax.
* Lists use asterisks or hash symbols.
* All macros are closed.
* Code indentation is preserved.
* No technical details were invented.
* Missing information uses visible placeholders.
* The section hierarchy is logical.
* The output is immediately paste-ready.